# SRG-APP-000116-WSR-000066  -  CAT II
# The web server must use the internal system clock to generate time stamps
# for log records.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000116-WSR-000066
group:           V-206367
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000159
control_rev5:    AU-8

contribution:    Splunk Web renders its own access log timestamps from the host clock and
                 that rendering is not configurable; what the application does configure is
                 whether splunkd stamps the indexed copy of those records itself rather
                 than accepting a time chosen by the input layer.

governs:         props.conf - How incoming data is recognized, parsed and timestamped.

check:           props.conf [<source type>] DATETIME_CONFIG not_equals NONE

external:        Accuracy and synchronization of the host clock that splunkd and Splunk Web
                 read - the operating system time service (NTP or chrony), which no Splunk
                 setting reaches
external:        The check's alternative workaround - generate a log entry, then query the
                 operating system clock and compare - which is a manual observation, not a
                 configuration read

citation:        web.conf [settings] log.access_file non_empty is removed. It names the
                 access log file (web.conf.spec:871-874, default web_access.log) and bears
                 on nothing in a timestamp requirement, and because the default supplies
                 the value, btool reports it non-empty on every instance, so the condition
                 cannot fail. A condition that cannot fail turns a requirement green
                 without evidence, which is worse than leaving the key out of the
                 criterion.
finding:         Splunk is configured with DATETIME_CONFIG = NONE for one or more source
                 types, so the application does not stamp those records from the system
                 clock it reads.
